<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Los Adaes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1721">1721</a>–<a href="/years/1773">1773</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Presidio Los Adaes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Los Adaes was a Spanish mission and presidio near present-day Robeline in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, founded in 1721 to block French expansion from Natchitoches. From 1729 to 1772 it served as the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded Louisiana to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the frontier post lost its purpose, and its settlers were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a> in 1773. The site is now a historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/los-adaes.jpg" alt="Los Adaes" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume de L'Isle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adaie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The settlement combined a Franciscan mission, San Miguel de Cuéllar de los Adaes, and a presidio, Nuestra Señora del Pilar de los Adaes. Its name comes from the Adai people, members of the Caddoan confederacy, whom the missionaries hoped to convert. It lay in disputed ground: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> held the Sabine River to be the western boundary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> claimed the Red River as the eastern boundary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, leaving an overlap of 45 miles. The presidio stood only 12 miles from the French post at Natchitoches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A mission of San Miguel de los Adaes was founded after Domingo Ramon's expedition of 1716. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-quadruple-alliance">War of the Quadruple Alliance</a>, in June 1719, seven Frenchmen from Natchitoches seized it from its sole defender, and the Spanish fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a>. The Marquis de San Miguel de Aguayo raised 500 soldiers to retake eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and in 1721 ordered the new presidio built, guarded by six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and 100 soldiers.</p><p>Spanish law forced goods for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> to travel by way of Vera Cruz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, so the settlers traded instead with the French at Natchitoches, and the Indians stayed loyal to French traders. The friendly Caddoan peoples did not convert, and the missionaries' college at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> recalled them in 1768. When the Marqués de Rubí inspected the post after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fontainebleau-1762">Treaty of Fontainebleau</a> of 1762, he found not a single Indian at the mission, twenty-five Spanish families on small ranches and sixty-one soldiers, and recommended abandoning eastern Texas.</p><p>In 1773 the settlers, by then about 500 people, a mixture of Spanish, French, Indians and a few black people, were given five days to leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a>. Many died on the three-month trek or soon after. After protests they were allowed back east in 1774, and following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> raids from 1779 they founded Nacogdoches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site lies in Natchitoches Parish near Robeline, preserved as the Los Adaes State Historic Site. It was designated a National Historic Landmark in 1986 and lies on the El Camino Real de los Tejas National Historic Trail. Excavations by Hiram F. Gregory Jr. of Northwestern State University from the 1960s to the 1980s made it an important site for the study of colonial Spanish and Adai culture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Los Adaes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1729 to 1772 the capital was <b>Los Adaes</b>, near the French border; it then moved to San Antonio de Béxar.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-texas">Spanish Texas</a></li></ul></section>
